{
  "_comment": "Quadrotor example \u2014 a fully COMPONENT-BUILT vehicle. There is no vehicle-level 'mass' block: the total mass, CG, and inertia are assembled automatically from each part (4 electric rotors + a structural airframe + a discrete battery mass) via the parallel-axis theorem. It showcases electric propulsion with ONE shared battery feeding all four motors, multi-component mass assembly with propeller gyroscopics, bluff-body (simple-geometry) aero, and a full onboard sensor suite (IMU/GPS/barometer/magnetometer) fused by the EKF. The controller flies on the EKF-ESTIMATED state (data_type ekf_both) to demonstrate control under sensor uncertainty. Geometry/propulsion/inertia from Phillips 'Simulation of Flight' Appendix C.4 (Tables C.4.1-C.4.3) and the electric motor/battery from Example 4.6.1. To fly it, run the quad PID controller src_quadrotorPID (bin/quadPID) — see controllers/README.md; for a live closed loop set realtime=true so the physics does not outrun the 100 Hz controller.",
  "simulation": {
    "time_step[s]": 0.002,
    "realtime": true,
    "realtime_note": "false for CMA tuning (runs as fast as the lockstep allows). Set true to fly/view live.",
    "end_time[s]": 600.0,
    "end_time_note": "short per-run window for tuning; raise (e.g. 1000) for live flying.",
    "save_states": true,
    "save_states_rate[hz]": 100,
    "geographic_model": "flat",
    "print_states_rate[hz]": 0.2
  },
  "atmosphere": {
